Here's an example of how you can define the interfaces:

```java
public interface ConversationEngine {
    void processMessage(Message message);
    void manageLineage(Message message);
}

public interface ContextEngine {
    Reference selectReference();
    void budgetReference(Reference reference);
}

public interface ApplicationOrchestrator {
    void coordinateEngines(Message message);
}
```

You can then create separate classes for each engine that implement the interfaces. For example, you can create a class for the Conversation Engine that implements the `ConversationEngine` interface and provides the necessary implementation for the `processMessage` and `manageLineage` methods.

```java
public class ConversationEngineImpl implements ConversationEngine {
    @Override
    public void processMessage(Message message) {
        // Process the message
    }

    @Override
    public void manageLineage(Message message) {
        // Manage the lineage of the message
    }
}
```

You can also create separate classes for each engine that implement the interfaces. For example, you can create a class for the Context Engine that implements the `ContextEngine` interface and provides the necessary implementation for the `selectReference` and `budgetReference` methods.

```java
public class ContextEngineImpl implements ContextEngine {
    @Override
    public Reference selectReference() {
        // Select a reference
        return new Reference();
    }

