        // Budget the reference
    }
}
```

Finally, you can create separate classes for each engine that implement the interfaces. For example, you can create a class for the Application Orchestrator that implements the `ApplicationOrchestrator` interface and provides the necessary implementation for the `coordinateEngines` method.

```java
public class ApplicationOrchestratorImpl implements ApplicationOrchestrator {
    @Override
    public void coordinateEngines(Message message) {
        // Coordinate the engines
    }
}
```

You can then use the interfaces to coordinate the engines. For example, you can create an instance of the `ConversationEngineImpl`, `ContextEngineImpl`, and `ApplicationOrchestratorImpl` classes and use the interfaces to coordinate the engines.

```java
ConversationEngine conversationEngine = new ConversationEngineImpl();
